# Drizzle decides which migrations are already applied by hashing the CONTENTS of
# each .sql file. With core.autocrlf=true a Windows checkout gets CRLF, which
# changes every one of those hashes — so a server run from a Windows working tree
# looks at a fully-migrated database, matches almost nothing, and tries to re-apply
# the entire history onto existing tables:
#
#   WARN  ... had drifted migration history; repaired migration journal entries
#   INFO  Applying 193 pending migrations
#   ERROR relation "agent_runtime_state" already exists
#
# The published npm package is packed on Linux and ships LF, so its hashes match
# and it reports "Migrations: already applied" against the same database. Pin .sql
# to LF so a local build agrees with the database the published build migrated.
*.sql text eol=lf
